# Shared declarations for every ad-hoc landmark staging publish model.
#
# One `{code}_landmarks_staging.preql` per curated-CSV city, each run by its
# own `landmarks-{code}` [[cloud.job]] in ../trilogy.toml. This file holds
# what they all share; a city's own file is just its CSV source and the
# `copy into` that publishes it.
#
# The curated CSV is committed (raw/{code}/{city}_landmarks.csv, written by
# that city's `_landmarks_geocode.py`) and travels to the cloud workspace on
# every sync, so firing the job republishes it as the staging parquet the
# city's landmark model reads — with the job's HMAC credentials, no local
# Google credential anywhere. The flow after editing a CSV: merge, let the
# sync run, then `trilogy cloud jobs run urban-tree-landmarks-{code} --wait`.
#
# `copy into` under `operation = "run"` rather than a materialised datasource
# under `refresh`, deliberately: a refresh target needs a freshness watermark
# to ever rebuild once its object exists (verified — a no-freshness target is
# only built while missing), and the only honest watermark for "republish
# when a person fires the job" is the firing itself. `run` says exactly that:
# every execution copies, unconditionally. It is also why these jobs must
# never gain a frequent cron — republishing an unchanged CSV still moves the
# staging object's Last-Modified, which the city's landmark probe reads, and
# the city's landmark parquet would rebuild for nothing.
#
# Standalone like the OSM staging models: nothing here imports raw/, so no
# landmark refresh can adopt a staging asset. The concepts mirror the
# canonical landmark columns by name because the staging parquet's column
# names are the contract raw/{code}/{city}_landmarks.preql reads.

key landmark_id string;
property landmark_id.name string;
property landmark_id.city string;
property landmark_id.geometry_raw string;
property landmark_id.latitude float;
property landmark_id.longitude float;
